<h2>Quick answer</h2>
<p>Yes, there are loads of them. Right now, part-time jobs 16-year-olds can apply for are mostly fast-food crew, grocery store clerk, retail associate, and movie theater crew. The bit that surprises people is that federal law puts no limit on your hours at 16 or 17. Your state might, though — and a few jobs stay off limits until you're 18. Ready to start? <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/part-time-online-jobs-for-students-that-actually-fit-around-classes-20261021">See current part-time jobs on Growentix</a> and apply to two or three of them this week.</p>
<h2>Part-time jobs for 16-year-olds: what the law says</h2>
<p>Before you apply for part-time jobs 16-year-olds can take, get the rules straight — they decide what you can do, how long you can work, and whether you need working papers first.</p>

<p>The federal side? The Fair Labor Standards Act — the FLSA. Your state adds its own limits on top.</p>
<h3>Federal hours rules</h3>
<p> 16 is the basic minimum age under federal law for most jobs. Once you hit 16 or 17 there is no federal limit on the number of hours you can work or on the times of day you can work (the old rules for 14/15 year olds of no more than 3 hours on a school day, no more than 18 hours in a school week and no work before 7am or after 7pm all cease to you). However many states have their own rules for 16 and 17 year olds and the stricter of those will prevail.</p>
<h3>Jobs you cannot do before 18</h3>
<p>Federal law bans everyone under 18 from 17 hazardous occupations. The ones most likely to come up in a first-job search are: driving a motor vehicle on public roads as part of the job (16 year olds can't drive for work at all, 17-year-olds can only drive in very narrow circumstances in daylight), operating or cleaning power driven meat processing machines, bakery machines, woodworking machines, metal forming machines, saws and balers or compactors, roofing work, trenching and excavation, wrecking and demolition, mining, logging and sawmilling and work with explosives or radioactive materials. In plain terms? No delivery driving. No forklifts. No roofing. And none of the big machines in the back of a grocery store or a bakery. The good news — all of it opens up at 18, so part-time jobs 18-year-olds can include roles that are closed to you now.</p>
<h3>State rules: two examples</h3>
<p>Two states, two sets of numbers:</p>

<table>
<tr><td>New York</td><td>4 hours on a school day (8 hours on Fridays, Saturdays, Sundays and holidays), 28 hours in a school week. No work before 6 a.m. or after 10 p.m. on a night before a school day. School's out: 8 hours a day, 48 a week.</td></tr>
<tr><td>California</td><td>4 hours on a school day, 48 hours a week. Nothing before 5 a.m. or after 10 p.m. on a night before a school day.</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Other states have their own numbers — check your state labor department before you accept a schedule.</p>
<h3>Pay: what you can expect</h3>
<p>The federal minimum wage is $7.25 an hour, and workers under 20 can be paid a youth rate of $4.25 an hour for their first 90 days with an employer. In practice most 16 year olds are being paid more as most states have a higher minimum wage (California pays $16.90 an hour in 2026) and that higher rate will always be in force. The pay ranges in this guide are therefore state and employer specific.</p>
<h2>Working papers: do you need them?</h2>
<p>The federal position is that no work permits are required at all. But plenty of states do — and most part-time jobs 16-year-olds take in those states need one before you can start. If you're 16 or 17 you'll need an employment certificate or work permit in California, New York, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Massachusetts, Michigan, Indiana, Maryland, Delaware, Louisiana, Alabama, North Carolina, Washington, and the District of Columbia. Connecticut, Rhode Island, and Hawaii want an age certificate instead, at 16 and 17. In Texas, Florida, Arizona, Idaho, South Carolina, South Dakota, and several more, there's no permit needed at 16 (though your employer will still be required to keep proof of your age on file.</p>
<p>Getting one isn't hard — it just takes a few steps. First, get the job offer — the permit is tied to one employer. Your employer fills in the job and hours, a parent or guardian signs, and you provide proof of age (birth certificate, passport, or state ID). Who hands you the permit? Usually your school. In Maryland, Virginia, and North Carolina it's the state labor department, and New Jersey does the whole thing online. Start the week you get the offer — some states won't let you start work until the permit's approved.</p>
<h2>Part-time jobs 16-year-olds: 14 employers hiring now</h2>
<p>These part-time jobs 16-year-olds can get are all over the country. One warning: the minimum age varies from store to store — same chain, different franchise, different answer. Always check the posting for your store. If one store says no, try another location of the same chain. Pay's shown as a range — it varies by state and employer. You can also <a href="https://growentix.cloud/usa">browse USA part-time jobs</a> to compare openings near you.</p>
<h2>1. McDonald's — Crew member</h2>
<p>Front counter, drive-thru and kitchen prep. Pay is generally $10 to $16 an hour depending on your state and the restaurant. Most McDonald's hires at 16, and some hire at 14 or 15 where state law allows. No experience is required as you'll be given training. You can apply via the McDonald's careers site or in the restaurant.</p>
<h2>2. Chick-fil-A — Front-of-house team member</h2>
<p>Cashier and dining room work (taking orders, serving the guests, keeping the dining room clean). Pay is $11 to $17 an hour. Most places hire at 16, some at 14 or 15. Each restaurant is independently run so the operator will set their own hiring age. Apply via the restaurant's hiring page or in person.</p>
<h2>3. Starbucks — Barista</h2>
<p>Making drinks, taking orders, cleaning the store. Pay is typically $12 to $17 an hour, plus tips in some stores. The minimum age is 16 (though in Montana a few stores will hire at 14). Apply via the Starbucks careers site.</p>
<h2>4. Chipotle — Crew member</h2>
<p>Prepare food, serve customers and clean the restaurant. Pay is generally $12 to $17 an hour. You have to be at least 16 to work at Chipotle. Crew get free food and education benefits. Apply via the Chipotle careers site.</p>
<h2>5. Taco Bell — Team member</h2>
<p>Front counter, drive-thru and kitchen. Pay is generally $10 to $16 an hour. Most hire at 16. Apply via the Taco Bell careers site or in the restaurant.</p>
<h2>6. Wendy's — Crew member</h2>
<p>Front counter, drive-thru and kitchen prep. Pay is typically $10-$15 an hour. Most Wendy's hires at 16. Apply via the Wendy's careers site or in the restaurant.</p>
<h2>7. Dunkin' — Crew member</h2>
<p>Taking orders, making the coffee and the donuts and working the drive-thru. Pay is generally $10 to $15 an hour. Most Dunkin's hires at 16. Apply via the Dunkin' careers site or in the store.</p>
<h2>8. Subway — Sandwich artist</h2>
<p>Make sandwiches, take orders and keep the store clean. Pay is typically $10 to $15 an hour. Most stores hire at 16, but it varies by franchise. Apply in the store or on the Subway careers site.</p>
<h2>9. Walmart — Cashier and stocking associate</h2>
<p>Cashier, stocker, cart attendant, online order pickup. Pay is typically $12 to $17 an hour. Walmart hires 16-year-olds for select roles — driving and heavy equipment jobs require you to be 18. Apply via the Walmart careers site.</p>
<h2>10. Target — Guest advocate</h2>
<p>Cashier and sales floor work, cart attendant, and pulling online orders. Pay is typically $12 to $17 an hour. Target hires 16-year-olds for select roles, including guest advocate and fulfillment team member. Apply via the Target careers site.</p>
<h2>11. Kroger — Courtesy clerk and cashier</h2>
<p>Bagging, cashiering, stocking shelves, pulling online orders. Pay is generally $11 to $16 an hour. Kroger hires at 16; some stores hire baggers as young as 14 with a work permit. Apply via the Kroger careers site.</p>
<h2>12. Publix — Cashier and front service clerk</h2>
<p>Cashier, bagging and stocking in this southeastern grocery chain. Pay is typically $11 to $16 an hour. Publix employs cashiers and front service clerks from 14. Apply via the Publix careers site.</p>
<h2>13. AMC Theatres — Crew member</h2>
<p>Selling tickets, working concessions, cleaning theaters — that's the job. Pay is typically $9 to $15 an hour. AMC hires at 16, and some locations take 14-year-olds too. Oh, and crew often get free movies. Apply on the AMC careers site.</p>
<h2>14. Six Flags — Park team member</h2>
<p>Ride operations (most rides require you to be 16), food service, ticket sales and park services. Pay is generally $11 to $16 an hour. Six Flags hires 14-year-olds for basic roles, but many positions require you to be 16. Apply via the Six Flags careers site.</p>
<p>More places that hire at 16: H-E-B (16 for baggers and checkers), Regal Cinemas, Culver's, Burger King, Little Caesars, American Eagle, Best Buy, and Buffalo Wild Wings.</p>
<h2>Requirements and tips for part-time jobs for teens</h2>
<p>Most part-time jobs 16-year-olds apply for ask for the same few things. Be 16. Be free evenings or weekends. Be reliable. That's basically it — you don't need experience, because employers expect to train you. Two things to sort out before you apply: know your availability (managers hire the person who can work the shifts they need), and have your Social Security number ready, because you'll need it when you're hired. And be straight about your age and school hours. If your state caps you at 4 hours on a school day, say so up front. Turned down at one store? Apply at another location of the same chain — hiring ages and needs change from store to store. New openings pop up every week, so check the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/">Growentix jobs page</a> regularly.</p>
<h2>How to find and apply for part-time jobs 16-year-olds can get</h2>
<p>Start with the employers above and apply to three or four — most take online applications that take ten minutes. Walk in and ask for the hiring manager if you don't hear back in a week. Check new listings every few days, because part-time openings fill fast. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/usa">Browse USA part-time jobs</a> on Growentix to see what's open near you and <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/shiksha-flags-top-part-time-jobs-for-students-abroad-in-2027-20261019">see part-time jobs for students</a> if you want more ideas for balancing work with school.</p>
<p><strong>Never pay anyone for a job.</strong> A real employer will never ask you to pay for an application, a training fee, or a uniform before you start. Someone asks for money to get you hired? Walk away. It's a scam.</p>
<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/43-child-labor-non-agriculture">U.S. Department of Labor Fact Sheet #43</a> (federal youth employment rules, hours limits, and the 17 hazardous occupations); <a href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/state/age-certificates/2019">U.S. Department of Labor employment and age certificate table</a> (which states require working papers and where to get them); <a href="https://www.dol.gov/general/topic/wages/minimumwage">U.S. Department of Labor minimum wage</a> (the $7.25 federal minimum and the $4.25 youth rate for the first 90 days).</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Can a 16 year old work full time?</h3>
<p>Under federal law, there's no hours limit at 16 or 17. Most states do however limit your hours while school is in session (New York for instance caps school weeks at 28 hours). So how much you can work will be down to your state.</p>
<h3>What jobs hire at 16 with no experience?</h3>
<p>The easiest part-time jobs 16-year-olds can get with no experience are fast-food crew, grocery bagger and cashier, retail associate, and movie theater crew — none of them needs experience, and they all train you on the job. McDonald's, Chick-fil-A, Walmart, Target, Kroger, and AMC are good places to start.</p>
<h3>Do I need working papers at 16?</h3>
<p>It differs from state to state. California, New York, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, and a few others require an employment certificate at 16 and 17. Texas, Florida, Arizona, and a few others don't. If in doubt, check with your school office.</p>
<h3>How much do part time jobs for teens pay?</h3>
<p>Most pay $10 to $17 an hour (it varies by state and employer). The federal minimum is $7.25 but most states have higher rates (California is at $16.90 in 2026). Your first 90 days can be paid at the $4.25 youth rate if you're under 20.</p>
<h3>Can 16 year olds do online jobs?</h3>
<p>Mostly no — most online part-time jobs need you to be 18. Contracts, payments, age checks. A few work at 16 with a parent's help — tutoring younger kids, selling things you've made. When you turn 18, <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/20-companies-hiring-for-part-time-work-from-home-roles-20261010">see 20 companies hiring for part-time work-from-home roles</a>.</p>
<h3>What changes when I turn 18?</h3>
<p>At 18, the federal youth rules just... stop. No more hour limits. No more hazardous job bans. No more work permits. And that's when part-time jobs 18-year-olds can include delivery driving, warehouse work, and construction.</p>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Getting one of the part-time jobs 16-year-olds can apply for comes down to two things: start early, apply widely.</p>

<p>Pick three jobs that hire at 16. Check whether your state needs work permits. Then apply this week. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/part-time-online-jobs-for-students-that-actually-fit-around-classes-20261021">See current part-time jobs on Growentix</a> — and take the first step.</p>
